---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/guides/discount-discipline";
const title = "Shopify email discount discipline: govern the offer, measure contribution";
const desc = "Build an offer register, checkout enforcement and a contribution-based evaluation for lifecycle discounts.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Must the first cart message always avoid a discount?",
    "answer": "Choose based on the commercial purpose, existing promises and order economics. Reminder-only is a useful option, not a universal rule."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a unique code stop all discount leakage?",
    "answer": "No. Inspect customer, product, expiry and combination rules at checkout, including already-issued codes and mixed baskets."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does attributed revenue prove a discount worked?",
    "answer": "No. Evaluate contribution after variable costs and refunds, and use a suitable controlled comparison when feasible to assess additional purchases."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">Operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16 space-y-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Inventory promises before changing the ladder</h2>
      <p>List every active offer in popups, welcome messages, cart reminders, winback, subscription signup, affiliate links and scheduled campaigns. Include already-issued codes whose emails are no longer active. Record the exact promise, audience, products, minimum spend, expiry, stacking behavior and issuer. A flow being paused does not revoke a code already in a customer’s inbox.</p>
      <p>Capture promised benefits before replacing default templates. If a subscriber signed up for a stated incentive, quietly delaying or removing it breaks the capture-to-message handoff. Change future acquisition copy and downstream rules together. Route an existing promise that conflicts with a new policy to the commercial owner for resolution rather than inventing a different deadline.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Write an offer contract the checkout can enforce</h2>
      <p>For each offer, name the owner, eligible customer and item states, allowed combinations, maximum exposure and exit conditions. Use limits derived from product economics, contractual promises and the purpose of the campaign. No universal percentage cap, cart value floor or message number is suitable for every catalog.</p>
      <p>Email branching is only one layer. Verify the checkout rules for customer eligibility, product exclusions, minimum purchase, redemption limits and expiry. A unique code can still leak or apply to the wrong basket if enforcement is loose. Have a documented fallback when code generation fails: suppress the offer or show approved non-offer content, never send a broken placeholder.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Choose the job of each lifecycle offer</h2>
      <p>A cart reminder may resolve delivery cost, fit uncertainty or a payment issue without a discount. An incentive may be appropriate for an approved acquisition campaign, a service recovery promise or stock clearance. State the commercial hypothesis and the costs it may create instead of making “no discount in message one” a universal law.</p>
      <p>Welcome should honor its capture promise. Browse content should not imply a cart exists. Post-purchase offers should account for cancellations, returns and delivery problems. Winback should reflect the product’s actual buying cycle; delay alone does not prove a customer needs a deeper code. Preserve reasons for each branch so the next operator can review them.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Resolve overlapping offers before sending</h2>
      <p>Create a precedence decision for public sales, promised signup incentives, lifecycle offers and subscription terms. Decide whether an existing promise is honored, replaced with an equal permitted benefit, or handled by support when it cannot be reconciled. Do not tell a customer an offer is exclusive while advertising a better public one without explanation.</p>
      <p>Recheck purchases, current sale state and outstanding offers before dispatch. Stop queued discount follow-ups after a qualifying purchase. At campaign end, restore lifecycle eligibility using current state rather than replaying skipped offers. Keep a designated sending owner during migrations; two tools issuing different codes can defeat a perfectly documented ladder.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Calculate contribution on the same basis</h2>
      <p>For a proposed order, start with item revenue after discounts and expected refunds. Subtract product cost, variable fulfilment, payment fees, shipping subsidy, gift cost and variable communication cost. Keep taxes and shipping collections on a consistent accounting basis chosen with finance. Compare like-for-like baskets; a larger discounted basket may still contribute less after fulfilment.</p>
      <p>Free shipping is not inherently cheaper than a percentage offer: destination, basket weight and carrier charges matter. A gift has product and handling cost even if its stated retail value is high. Model low-margin items, mixed baskets, remote destinations and returns. Set eligibility using those cases instead of average order value alone.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Separate redemption reporting from causal evidence</h2>
      <p>An order using a code demonstrates redemption, not that the email caused an additional purchase. Platform attribution can assign revenue to customers who would have bought anyway. Track net contribution, repeat full-price orders, refunds and customer complaints alongside redemption and attributed revenue.</p>
      <p>If volume supports a meaningful experiment, define an eligible population, random assignment, observation window, exclusion rules and the outcome before launch. Compare a permitted offer with an appropriate alternative while honoring existing promises. Finance should choose a decision rule and account for uncertainty; no fixed holdout share or conversion gap proves discount training for every store.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Account for repeat purchase and customer mix</h2>
      <p>Compare cohorts with similar acquisition source, product and time since first purchase. A rise in discounted orders can reflect a clearance event or a different merchandise mix, not necessarily learned waiting behavior. Keep recent cohorts separate until they have had a realistic opportunity to reorder.</p>
      <p>Do not label every VIP a full-price buyer or every discount redeemer a bargain-only buyer. A loyalty benefit can be commercially justified, but define what it is paying for. Early access, service or product information may fit some customers better than a code; their operational costs and inventory promises still need an owner.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Handle catalog and subscription exceptions explicitly</h2>
      <p>Exclude products whose current margins or agreements make the offer unsuitable. Refresh eligibility after a price change, bundle revision or supplier-cost update. A cart containing an excluded item needs clear terms, not a surprise failure after the email says the entire cart qualifies.</p>
      <p>For subscriptions, distinguish an introductory purchase from later renewals and changes to an existing contract. Confirm billing-system support before promising a recurring saving. For fashion launches, check exact variant inventory before offering early access; for jewelry, financing language and made-to-order commitments need their own approval. Product type informs the offer contract without dictating one universal strategy.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Rehearse state changes and record actual results</h2>
      <p>Propose a controlled checkout exercise: a subscriber with a welcome promise enters cart recovery during a public sale, buys an excluded item, then returns to the old email after the sale ends. Inspect the accepted combination, customer-facing explanation, queued messages and code expiry. Repeat for a subscription basket and an issued code whose flow has been paused.</p>
      <p>These are suggested exercises, not tests performed for this guide. Save observed outputs and unresolved mismatches before release. Ask Sequenzy and other candidates to demonstrate how the operator finds outstanding codes, changes exclusions, pauses a branch and exports redemption data. Confirm plan and integration limits rather than assuming governance is built into every template.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Release changes with a reversible commercial handoff</h2>
      <p>First reconcile active promises and checkout enforcement. Then change a bounded branch with an owner, expected effect and observation window. Keep prior settings, code inventory and the affected audience definition available. Rollback must address both messaging and checkout; restoring old copy while leaving incompatible code rules creates another broken promise.</p>
      <p>Give finance and support a report of issued, redeemed, expired and unexpectedly combined offers, plus contribution by comparable cohort. Review exceptions when catalog costs, public promotions or billing terms change. Include platform, capture, integration and staff costs when comparing app options. A migration needs a concrete operational benefit and measured economics, not a predetermined multiple of labor cost.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <nav class="container pb-12 flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related guidance">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">App evaluations</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/consent-compliance">Consent</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/deliverability">Deliverability</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/discount-discipline">Discount discipline</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/migration">Migration</a>
  </nav>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
